# The documentation flow — how every generated document in this repository
# is made, checked and assembled into the site, as ONE flow document
# (RULED: CICD-1; platform/flow.md §2.1, §4.15, §4.16).
#
# One document, three callers, one command line each:
#
#   make docs          the developer's regeneration   --mode=write
#   make test-docs     the post-merge doc pipeline    --mode=check (INT-10)
#   .github/workflows/site.yml, before the Pages upload   --mode=check
#
# all three through `make docs-flow`, which builds the toolchain and then runs
#
#   cx flow run docs-src/flow/docs.flow.cx --env docs-src/flow/docs.env.cx \
#     --ephemeral --mode=write|check --allow-read --allow-write \
#     --allow-subprocess --allow-env
#
# A flow document is DATA: a closed vocabulary (§2.3), no [?lib] of its own,
# every act named by qualified name in the canonical [do 'ns/verb' …] form.
# The acts are docs-src/flow/docs-acts.cx, each a make target underneath, and
# every target the flow runs is NAMED here, in its act's fields — the one list
# a reader, the step roster (contributor_facts.cx) and this flow share.
#
# THE ORDER. generate → check → assemble. `needs=` says it where document
# order already would, so the DAG is read off the document rather than
# inferred from its layout (§4.19).
#
# THE PIVOT IS GENERATE (§4.7). Regeneration overwrites tracked files and no
# act puts the previous bytes back, so it is the flow's irreversible point and
# nothing precedes it — which is why no act here owes a [compensates]
# pairing: the only position that would owe one is empty. A red check or a
# red assembly after it lands the run `:incomplete` with the count visible,
# and the regenerated files stay for the developer to read; a red generate
# lands it `:compensated` with nothing to undo. Either way the record names
# the step and carries the red target's tail — the one place to read it.
#
# --ephemeral, not the checkout's file:// journal. The run id is derived from
# (document, actor, args), so under a durable journal a second `make docs`
# with the same arguments would answer `[deduped …]` and regenerate nothing.
# A docs run is one-shot by nature; its record is printed, and the exit code
# is the CLI's (0 `:done`, 1 anything else).
#
# DOCS-4 (RULED: DOCS-42, DOCS-43, DOCS-44, DOCS-45): the generate step's
# docs-generate renders the ring figure from the registries (ring-svg) beside
# the landing page that inlines it; the check step carries docs-voice-check
# (the docs speak for CX, DOCS-43) and ring-svg-check (the figure's module
# ticks equal the registry's count); the internal link walk needs nothing here
# — it is inside site-check (DOCS-44); the external-link fetch (DOCS-45) is
# NOT a step of this document, which also runs pre-merge and post-merge
# offline: it is the Site workflow's own step after this flow.
#
# PUBLISH IS NOT A STEP. The Site workflow deploys site/ with GitHub's Pages
# actions (upload-pages-artifact, deploy-pages), which authenticate with the
# job's OIDC token; an act cannot perform that deploy without re-implementing
# the Pages artifact API, so the flow ends at the assembled, listed site and
# the workflow's two Pages steps follow it, on cx main only.
[flow name="docs" stall-after=2h
  [args [mode::string]]
  [step name="generate" pivot=true
        [do 'docs/generate' [mode $args/mode] [target 'docs-generate']]]
  [step name="check" needs="generate"
        [do 'docs/check'
            [targets 'docs-check primer-platform-check guide-check verify-doc-links verify-doc-blocks verify-readme-blocks docs-voice-check ring-svg-check']]]
  [step name="assemble" needs="check"
        [do 'docs/assemble' [target 'site-check']]]]
